{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":50655355,"ns":0,"title":"Algebra extension","extract":"In abstract algebra, an algebra extension is the ring-theoretic equivalent of a group extension.\nPrecisely, a ring extension of a ring R by an abelian group I is a pair (E, \n  \n    \n      \n        ϕ\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle \\phi }\n  \n) consisting of a ring E and a ring homomorphism \n  \n    \n      \n        ϕ\n      \n    \n    {\\displaystyle \\phi }\n  \n that fits into the short exact sequence of abelian groups:\n\n  \n    \n      \n        0\n        →\n        I\n        →\n        E\n        \n          \n            \n              \n\n              \n              →\n              \n\n              \n            \n            ϕ\n          \n        \n        R\n        →\n        0.","description":"Surjective ring homomorphism with a given codomain","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}